# ============================================================================
# VALUES TUTORIAL (WITHOUT PLACES) - identity is content, change is a pair
# ============================================================================
#
# A store keeps a value at a PLACE - an address, a row number, a cell -
# and lets you come back to the place. Identity hangs on the place:
# "update" means same place, new content; a duplicate is one content at
# two places; NULL is a content that means "no content".
#
# In this algebra a value has no place. It is identified by what it says,
# and that identity is decided by the engine. Storage does not vanish -
# bytes are still written, hashed and signed - but it moves OUTSIDE the
# algebra, to the provenance layer (part 1c is about exactly that seam).
# Inside there are only values and the relations between them. This file
# is about what that buys: identity becomes a verdict, an update becomes
# a pair with a provable type, a duplicate becomes impossible, "history"
# becomes a question the algebra can answer, and a missing value becomes
# a degree instead of a hole.
#
# The notation is small. A stream is a named time series (o1, o2, ...);
# `o1[t]=1` is the sentence "stream o1 is 1 at every step". Braces turn
# a sentence into a VALUE: `{ o1[t]=1 }` is that sentence taken as an
# element of the algebra, so it can be met (&), joined (|), complemented
# ('), compared (=) and quantified over (all x:tau, ex x:tau). Every
# query below is one such sentence about values, answered T or F.
#
# Two neighbours got half of this each: event sourcing keeps the
# append-only half (state is derived, history is the truth), git keeps
# the content-addressed half (a thing is named by what it is). Neither
# can DECIDE that two different histories are the same value, or that
# a change is a tightening - here that is one query. Five parts: what a
# value is (0), identity is a verdict - values, not bytes (1), change is
# a pair with a type, and values
# remember their path exactly where order matters (2), counting is a
# sentence exactly when the roster is one (3), and the difference between
# two rule sets is itself a value - and a lossless one (4).
#
# Run with (minimal cost):
#   TAU_BA_COMPONENT_FACTORING=1 tau -q < values_without_places.tau
# Needs a build with ADT definitions (2026-09-02+); verified against
# 0.7.0-alpha, build 1c1e58ae.
#
# READING THE OUTPUT: "%N: ..." lines answer the n queries - consecutive
# IDENTICAL results share one %N slot, so match the answers by ORDER, not
# by number (annotations say "Answer:"). The closing run prints tuple wire
# lines oc[k] := { keeps: "...", gives_up: "..." } whose expected content
# is annotated at the run - the machine contract covers the %N lines. (The
# wire lines below the run are the last lines of the file on purpose: a
# tuple console stream reads until end-of-file.)
# ============================================================================

# EXPECTED-RESULTS: T T F T T F T F T F T F T T F T F T T

set charvar off
set maxsplits 1

# ============================================================================
# PART 0: WHAT A VALUE IS - the floor, the ceiling, and the room between
# ============================================================================
#
# A value here is a SENTENCE about streams - "o1 is 1", "o1 implies o2",
# a whole rule set. The algebra orders these sentences: a value that says
# MORE lies LOWER (it pins the streams tighter). At the bottom sits 0, the
# contradiction - one value for every impossible law. At the top sits 1,
# the sentence that says nothing - one value for the empty law. A freshly
# named stream carries exactly that: no sentence yet, the value 1, total
# freedom; meaning arrives only by legislation, one sentence at a time.
# And between the two ends there is room - this algebra is atomless, so
# between "pinned" and "free" there is always something strictly in
# between.

n ex x:tau (x != 0 && x != 1)
# Answer: T - values strictly between the floor and the ceiling exist.
# That room is where partial commitments, undecided cells and discretion
# live; a two-valued store has no such place.

n (({ o1[t]=1 } & { o1[t]=0 }) = 0)
# Answer: T - two sentences that cannot both hold meet in THE impossible
# value. There is one floor, and every contradiction is it.

n (({ o1[t]=1 } & { o1[t]=1 }) = 0)
# Answer: F - the control: a sentence with itself is not a contradiction.
# The floor is reached by conflict, not by repetition.

n (({ o1[t]=1 } | { o1[t]=1 }') = 1)
# Answer: T - a sentence joined with its complement says nothing at all:
# that is the ceiling, the empty law, the value a stream has before any
# rule touches it.
#
# One warning that other series taught: 0 and 1 mean OPPOSITE things
# depending on which way you accumulate. Law accumulates by MEET (each
# rule constrains): 1 is "no law yet", 0 is "contradiction". A table
# accumulates by JOIN (each row extends): 0 is "empty", 1 is "everything".
# Same lattice, read from both ends. And the rule for what becomes a value
# at all: a sentence about streams. A fact about the world (who a person
# is, what a word means) or a fact about the set of sentences (how many)
# never does - that is the seam the whole file keeps returning to.

# ============================================================================
# PART 1: IDENTITY IS A VERDICT - no address, no serial, no duplicate
# ============================================================================
#
# The algebra these values live in has had a name since the 1930s: the
# Lindenbaum-Tarski algebra - sentences taken modulo provable
# equivalence. "Identity is a verdict" is not a slogan about this engine;
# it is that algebra's definition. Two sentences that prove each other are
# one element by construction, and the engine decides the construction.

# --- 1a. What a logician derives, the algebra identifies ---
#
# In a store, "A -> B" and "A" are two rows; "A and B" is a third, and
# some program has to derive it. Here they are not three things:

n (({ o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1 } & { o1[t]=1 }) = { o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=1 })
# Answer: T - the rule "1 implies 2" together with the fact "1" IS the
# value "1 and 2". Modus ponens is not an inference step here; it is an
# identity of values. Nothing was derived and stored - there was never a
# second value.

n (({ o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1 } & { o2[t]=1 }) = { o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=1 })
# Answer: F - and the fallacy every logic course warns about (affirming
# the consequent: from "1 implies 2" and "2", conclude "1") is simply NOT
# an identity. The control is a classical mistake, and the verdict
# declines it.

# --- 1b. Leibniz, decided: indiscernible means identical ---
#
# Without addresses, what makes two values the same? Only their
# relations. Yes, the theorem below is antisymmetry of the order - the
# point is that it is the ONLY identity criterion there is. There is no
# address to fall back on, so this axiom carries the whole weight that a
# row number carries elsewhere:

n all a:tau all b:tau (((a & b') = 0 && (b & a') = 0) -> a = b)
# Answer: T - two values that entail each other ARE one value, for every
# pair there will ever be. (Corollary you can feel: inserting a row that
# is already present changes nothing - tables are sets because values have
# no places, not because someone deduplicated.)

n ex a:tau ex b:tau ((a & b') = 0 && (b & a') = 0 && a != b)
# Answer: F - the control: mutual containment with distinctness has no
# witness. The Adabas ISN and the SQL rowid answer a question that does
# not arise here.

# --- 1c. Two identities - bytes and values - and why you need both ---
#
# A journal of rule changes still has to be tamper-evident, and for that
# you hash BYTES (canonical serialisation, a hash chain, signatures): a
# byte is a place after all, and a chain of hashes guards places. But
# bytes are the wrong identity for the LAW. Any two builds, printers or
# serialisers may emit the same accumulated law with its clauses in a
# different order; a byte comparison calls that a change, a value
# comparison - the only identity that matters for what the law MEANS -
# calls it the same law. The two must not be confused, and both are
# needed: guard the bytes for provenance; trust the values for meaning.

n ({ o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=1 && o3[t]=0 } = { o3[t]=0 && o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=1 })
# Answer: T - three clauses, permuted: a different byte string, the same
# value. Provenance would call this a change; meaning does not.

n ({ o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=1 && o3[t]=0 } = { o3[t]=0 && o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=0 })
# Answer: F - and one flipped clause is a different value, however
# similar the bytes look. The verdict tracks meaning, not spelling.

# ============================================================================
# PART 2: CHANGE IS A PAIR WITH A TYPE - and values remember their path
# ============================================================================
#
# A value cannot change - it has no place to change in. What changes is
# which value stands at a stream position, so every change is a PAIR
# (old, new), and a pair of values has a decidable relation: the change
# has a TYPE, and the type is a verdict, not a diff someone eyeballed.

n (({ o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=1 } & { o1[t]=1 }') = 0)
# Answer: T - the new rule set lies within the old one: this change is a
# TIGHTENING (everything the old forbade stays forbidden). Read as
# governance: this is the shape a change must have wherever some core of
# the rules is meant to be irrevocable. And read as law: "lies within" is the order
# that Kelsen's hierarchy of norms calls validity - a bylaw is valid when
# it lies within its constitution. The Stufenbau is this lattice order,
# read as law.

n (({ o1[t]=1 } & { o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=1 }') = 0)
# Answer: F - the reverse pair is NOT a tightening: going from the
# two-clause set to the one-clause set allows what was forbidden. Same
# two values, opposite order - the type flips, and the verdict knows.

n ex a:tau ex b:tau ((a & b') != 0 && (b & a') != 0)
# Answer: T - and a third type exists: two rule sets that each allow
# something the other forbids. Incomparable changes are real, and they
# are the ones that need a decision rather than a proof.

# --- 2d. Values remember their path - exactly where order matters ---
#
# "No places" invites a wrong conclusion: that only the final
# value counts and history is gone. Not so. Whether a value carries its
# history depends on whether the change rule commutes - and that, too, is
# decided. Two ways of change: a MEET (adopt a rule into the set) and a
# REPLACE (start over from a protected core plus a new rule).

n ((({ o3[t]=0 } & { o1[t]=1 }) & { o2[t]=1 }) = (({ o3[t]=0 } & { o2[t]=1 }) & { o1[t]=1 }))
# Answer: T - two histories of adoptions in different order end in ONE
# value: meets commute, so the value forgets the order - two replicas
# that adopted the same rules in any order agree, and the equality is
# decided, not assumed.

n (({ o3[t]=0 } & { o1[t]=1 }) = (({ o3[t]=0 } & { o1[t]=1 }) & { o2[t]=1 }))
# Answer: F - but adopt-then-replace and replace-then-adopt do NOT end in
# the same value. Read the two sides as the two histories: on the left,
# adopt q and then REPLACE with p - a replace restarts from the core, so
# its result is core & p and carries no trace of q; on the right, replace
# with p and then adopt q - core & p & q. A replace from the core forgets
# what was adopted before it. A meet remembers, a replace forgets - and which operations
# are which is not a design note, it is decided per pair. Values carry
# their path exactly where the algebra proves order matters.

# ============================================================================
# PART 3: COUNTING IS A SENTENCE - exactly when the roster is one
# ============================================================================
#
# A Boolean algebra has no notion of "how many". So how did the
# constitution examples count a quorum? Because over a FIXED roster the
# members are named streams, and a sum over named streams is a sentence
# about streams - not a fact about a set. Three voters, two needed:

type Votes = {a: bv[8], b: bv[8], c: bv[8]}

n ex v:Votes (v.a <= { #x01 }:bv[8] && v.b <= { #x01 }:bv[8] && v.c <= { #x01 }:bv[8] && v.a + v.b + v.c >= { #x02 }:bv[8])
# Answer: T - a 2-of-3 quorum is reachable: counting lives INSIDE the
# algebra as bitvector arithmetic over three named members.

n ex v:Votes (v.a <= { #x01 }:bv[8] && v.b <= { #x01 }:bv[8] && v.c <= { #x01 }:bv[8] && v.a + v.b + v.c >= { #x04 }:bv[8])
# Answer: F - four of three is unsatisfiable, as it should be. The
# arithmetic is exact because the roster is.
#
# And the honest seam, stated once: over an OPEN population - voters that
# keep arriving - "how many" is not a sentence about streams but a fact
# about the set of sentences. That half belongs to a database (which
# counts) while the algebra judges. Any large system splits along this
# seam: judge in the algebra, count outside.

# ============================================================================
# PART 4: THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN TWO RULE SETS IS A VALUE - a lossless one
# ============================================================================
#
# If a change is a pair (old, new), then "what the change gave up" is
# old & new' - a value like any other. First the theorem that makes it
# more than a diff: for a tightening, yesterday is REBUILT from today plus
# what was given up. Nothing is lost, because the loss is a value.

n all a:tau all b:tau (((b & a') = 0) -> ((b | (a & b')) = a))
# Answer: T - for every tightening old -> new: new joined with what it
# gave up is old again, exactly. Yes, as algebra this is "b together with
# a-minus-b is a" - the point is what it is applied to: yesterday's LAW
# is recoverable from today's law plus one value, with no log, for every
# pair, not sampled.

n ex a:tau ex b:tau ((b | (a & b')) != a)
# Answer: T - the control that keeps the theorem honest: without the
# tightening condition, reconstruction can fail (a loosening gave nothing
# up, and joining nothing does not bring the old clauses back). The T
# above is earned by its condition, not free.

# ============================================================================
# WHERE THIS LEADS
# ============================================================================
#
# Every database is a machine for remembering WHERE things are. This
# algebra cannot remember where anything is, because there is no where -
# and so it is forced to remember what things ARE, and to prove what
# became of them. The practical half follows: a cached derived value - a
# snapshot of accumulated rules kept as if it were the truth - is a place
# holding a content again, and it is the one kind of thing this algebra
# cannot vouch for; the honest move is to re-derive it from the history
# and let the engine judge. The rule reaches all the way up: when the
# engine itself changes version, the old reading and the new reading of
# the same rules are compared as values before either is trusted. Even a
# change of the judge is a pair with a verdict. Values with a verdict
# behind them need no rescue; a stored value without one is exactly what
# this file has no room for.
#
# A store remembers where things are. This algebra remembers what things
# are - and proves what became of them.

# --- The closing run ---
#
# One structured input per step ({old, new}), one structured output
# ({keeps, gives_up}), equation form:
#   keeps[t]    = (new[t] & old[t]')'    T exactly when new forbids everything
#                                        old forbade - the tightening test
#   gives_up[t] = old[t] & new[t]'       the freedom the change takes away
# No diff tool, no change log: the relation between yesterday's law and
# today's is computed as a value, and when it is not simply T or F the
# printed formula IS the answer - it names exactly what changed.
#
# Expected wire output:
#   oc[0]: old = {o1=1}, new = {o1=1 && o2=1}. keeps T (a tightening);
#          gives_up = a formula - the models that the old set allowed and
#          the new one no longer does (o1 pinned to 1, o2 no longer pinned
#          to 1 at every step). Read it as
#          "this is the freedom you just gave up", spelled out - and by
#          the theorem above, old = new | gives_up.
#   oc[1]: the reverse pair. keeps = a formula, NOT T - something once
#          forbidden is now allowed, and the value names it; gives_up F -
#          nothing was taken away.
#   oc[2]: the same two clauses in a different order. keeps T, gives_up F:
#          reordering is not a change, because there was never a place
#          whose content moved - only a value, and it is the same value.
#
# (Printed formulas are normal forms and may differ in order between
# builds; their T/F status is the contract.)

type Step = {old: tau, new: tau}. type Rel = {keeps: tau, gives_up: tau}. ic:Step := in console. oc:Rel := out console. run (oc[t].keeps = (ic[t].new & ic[t].old')') && (oc[t].gives_up = ic[t].old & ic[t].new')
{ old: "o1[t]=1.", new: "o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=1." }
{ old: "o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=1.", new: "o1[t]=1." }
{ old: "o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=1.", new: "o2[t]=1 && o1[t]=1." }
